  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM EDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\n- Areas of Fog this Morning\n\nResidual moisture from rain over the past 24 hours, clearing\nbehind the showers in eastern Michigan, along with fog that has \ndeveloped over the cooler waters of Lake Michigan will bring \nreduced visibility to portions of western Michigan this morning. \nLowest visibilities of 0 to 1/2 mile are along Lake Michigan south\nof Grand Haven. Expect these lower visibilities to continue into \nThursday morning before improving within a few hours of sunrise. \nFog is not expected to be as dense further inland but could see \nsome spots drop down to 1 mile. Make sure to give yourself extra\ntime for the morning commute and use your low-beam headlights.\n\n- 20 to 30 Percent Chance for Showers and Thunderstorms Today\n\nOverall Thursday morning could remain largely dry with a diurnal \ntrend for showers and thunderstorms today. There will also be \nsome support with upper level divergence and a 500mb shortwave \npassing to the north during the afternoon. Showers and \nthunderstorms will be isolated to scattered during the afternoon \nand early evening before the inversion builds in. Instability \nbuilds to around 1000 J/kg this afternoon and with marginal shear\naround 25 knots. With these variables not expecting severe \nstorms, but the strongest storms could contain small hail and \ngusty winds.\n\n- Additional Rain and Thunderstorm Chances Friday into Next Week\n\nOverall the large scale pattern remains stationary with the upper\nlevel low anchored over the Hudson Bay. What that means for us is\ntroughing remains overhead with multiple shortwaves moving \nthrough into next week. With each disturbance in our moist \nenvironment, showers and thunderstorms will be possible. However, \nthere are differences with the timing and track of each of these \nshortwaves. This brings higher uncertainty with when and where the\nbest chances will be.\n\nAt this time there is better agreement for Friday into Saturday.\nFriday looks to be a rinse and repeat of today with some diurnal\nshowers and thunderstorms during the afternoon and early evening.\nA quick moving front tracks through the area Friday night into\nSaturday morning bringing another 20 to 30 percent chance for\nshowers and thunderstorms. Afterwards confidence quickly\ndecreases, but no one day looks like a washout as each shortwave\nquickly moves through. Additionally between the different severe\nweather outlook models, severe weather doesn't look likely over\nthe next week. Confidence should increase as we get closer, but it\nmay be an pattern where we have to take it day by day.",
